<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Falun Mine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Falun copper mine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Falun Mine was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mine in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, worked for many centuries. In the seventeenth century Sweden was the world's largest copper producer, and the Falun mine's output was for decades the largest single source of revenue for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>. The exhaustion of its richest ores later reduced this income.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/falun-mine.jpg" alt="Falun Mine" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arild Vågen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Falu_koppargruva_July_2017_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Falun Mine (Falu Gruva) operated for a millennium, from the 10th century to 1992. No written account fixes when mining began; archaeological and geological studies indicate, with considerable uncertainty, that it started around the year 1000. Objects of the 10th century contain its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, and the mine was certainly working by 1080. At first local farmers gathered the ore, smelted it and used the metal for household needs. Around the reign of Magnus III, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1275 to 1290, nobles and merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> took over, and the first written document, of 1288, records that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Västerås acquired a 12.5 percent interest in exchange for an estate.</p><p>By the middle of the 14th century the mine had become a vital national resource. King Magnus IV visited the area and drafted a charter, in 1347, that secured the sovereign's financial interest. Under it free miners held shares in proportion to the smelters they owned, a structure regarded as a precursor of the joint stock company. Output peaked in the 17th century, with over 3,000 tonnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> in 1650, and the Privy Council called the mine the nation's treasury and stronghold. It helped fund <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s wars in its great power era, and Sweden held a virtual monopoly on copper throughout the century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The main method was fire-setting: fires lit at the end of the day heated the rock, which cracked when it cooled and was broken loose with wedges and sledge hammers, so that the miners advanced about 1 metre a month. The ore was roasted in open hearths, producing a thick, poisonous smoke, and then smelted; the cycle was repeated until crude <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> resulted. The process changed little for seven centuries. On Midsummer's Eve 1687, after decades of intensive digging, a large part of the mine collapsed; no one was killed, because the miners did not work on that day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a> described the mine as one of the great wonders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> but as horrible as hell itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> output fell to less than 2,000 tonnes by 1665 and barely 1,000 tonnes a year between 1710 and 1720. The mining company turned to iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and began producing Falu red paint in earnest. Gold was found in 1881, leading to a short-lived gold rush and a total of 5 tonnes. The last shot was fired on 8 December 1992. Chemists found new elements in its minerals, among them selenium, isolated in 1817 by Jöns Jakob Berzelius and Johan Gottlieb Gahn. The mine is now a museum, owned by the Stora Kopparberget foundation, and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2001, together with the town of Falun, its 17th-century miners' cottages and the surrounding Bergsmansbygden.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Falun Mine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mining shaped the economy from the thirteenth century: the copper mountain of <b>Falun</b> and the iron districts of Bergslagen drew German capital and miners, and Stockholm grew as a port with a strong Hanseatic presence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The great <b>Falun copper mine</b> made Sweden Europe's dominant copper producer for much of the seventeenth century and was for decades a leading source of crown revenue, while Swedish bar iron supplied much of northern Europe's demand; the decline of the richest Falun ores later in the century…</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
